INT. FIELD HOSPITAL - SHIPPING CONTAINER OPERATING ROOM - NIGHT

Steel walls, painted white. Two surgical lamps on a generator hum. A battered table. A rack of salvaged instruments. A tarpaulin flaps outside. Gunfire, far off.

DR. HANNELORE BRIGG, 50s, mask, gown, hair under a cap, works over a patient's abdomen, hands deep, eyes steady. CASIMIR ODUYA, 30s, nurse, sweat on his forehead, holds a retractor and a flashlight in one hand and a suction line in the other.

HANNELORE
Suction.

CASIMIR
Suction.

HANNELORE
Clamp.

CASIMIR
Clamp.

He passes it. She takes it without looking.

HANNELORE
Light left.

He tilts the flashlight. The beam finds the wound.

HANNELORE (CONT'D)
Good.

The generator coughs. The lamps dim and recover. They both freeze, eyes on the bulbs. The hum steadies. They exhale at the same time.

CASIMIR
Fuel is low.

HANNELORE
How long?

CASIMIR
Twenty minutes.

HANNELORE
Enough.

CASIMIR
Doctor, I have a pulse of one-twenty.

HANNELORE
I know.

CASIMIR
Pressure's dropping.

HANNELORE
I know, Casimir.

She reaches, clamps, cauterizes. Smoke. He wipes her forehead with the back of his glove. She doesn't flinch.

HANNELORE (CONT'D)
Suture.

CASIMIR
Suture.

She sews. His thumb steadies the tissue. Her needle dips, rises, dips. A tide of small, precise movements.

CASIMIR (CONT'D)
Pulse ninety.

HANNELORE
Better.

CASIMIR
Pressure rising.

HANNELORE
Good.

She ties the last knot. Cuts it. Sets down the scissors. They stand a moment, both looking at the sleeping chest rise and fall.

CASIMIR
Doctor.

HANNELORE
Mm.

CASIMIR
Next.

HANNELORE
Next.

They pull off gloves, together, and drop them in the bucket.
